{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Change 2685 MERGED — the MEMORY half of this ticket is fixed. The ticket stays OPEN.\n\nSubmitted 2026-09-05 22:29:25Z, `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`, no `--force`.\n\"Reduce discovery reads without the bodies\" — the per-ticket projection inside `reduce_all_tickets`.\n\nMeasured effect: **~295 MB -> ~84 MB per concurrent whole-store read (3.5x)**, moving the 40-slot\nceiling from ~11.9 GB to ~3.4 GB — inside the 7.6 GiB host rather than above it.\n\n## Why this ticket does NOT close\n\nThe investigation established that this ticket's symptom — persistent HTTP 502 from the MCP\nendpoint — has **TWO INDEPENDENT CAUSES**, and 2685 addresses only the first:\n\n1. **Memory (FIXED by 2685).** `reduce_all_tickets` materialised every ticket's full compiled state\n   at once (243.4 MB for 8,879 tickets), offloaded through a 40-slot `anyio` limiter, giving\n   `RSS = 69 MB + K x 295 MB`. That bound EXCEEDED the host, so it OOM-ed before it could plateau —\n   which is why it presented as unbounded growth. Note the ticket's original LEAK premise was\n   REFUTED: `tracemalloc` showed Python retains nothing (a released compile returns to 13.9 MB every\n   time); the retainer is the allocator, which does not return freed arenas.\n2. **Latency (NOT fixed — `lousy-unsuited-gnu` / `94c5-b42e-ac80-4f06`, p1).** `resolve_ticket_id`\n   falls through to `_scan_alias` (`src/rebar/_ids.py:312`), which walks the store and parses every\n   ticket's CREATE/SNAPSHOT — roughly 8,900 JSON parses to resolve ONE id. A single-ticket read\n   therefore costs more than compiling the whole store.\n\n**Fixing (1) does not fix (2).** A `show_ticket` that costs a full-store scan will keep crossing the\nclient deadline whenever the host is contended, and a crossed deadline is not merely a slow read —\nit silently DROPS WRITES. Three agents independently hit `comment_ticket` and `create_ticket`\ntimeouts tonight where the write never landed and the local CLI completed the identical write\ninstantly.\n\n## Remaining work on this ticket\n\n- AC5 still needs steady-state per-container RSS and the RSS-vs-ticket-size relationship, both of\n  which require on-box data rather than gate telemetry.\n- Plan-review retention (`relation_snapshot.py:257`, held 15-20 min) was never driven during the\n  investigation and is filed UNMEASURED as `3499-ee9f`.\n\n## A measurement caveat that must not be lost\n\nThe latency figures gathered during this work (41.7 s idle `show_ticket`, 16.3 s whole-store read,\n80.5 s under 8-way concurrency) are **CONFOUNDED**: \"idle\" meant no in-flight MCP requests, not a\nquiet host, and host load was ~22-26 at the time. They are not a baseline.\n\nWhat survives the caveat: the MECHANISM (read from source, not timed), the **2.0x ratio** between\nsingle-ticket and whole-store reads (measured back-to-back under identical ambient load), and every\nMEMORY number (peak RSS for a given K is unaffected by CPU contention). What does NOT survive is the\nsharper claim that `show_ticket` sits \"already at the edge\" of the 60 s deadline — the quiet-host\nfloor may be lower and the margin larger. Re-measure in-process on an unloaded host before anyone\nleans on that."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788647407560964001,"uuid":"4bf20c95-c451-4c00-a538-cda85ec1dfed"}